 // ===========================================================================
 // Host-driven input path for embedded use.
 // ===========================================================================
 //
 // Why this file exists
 // --------------------
 // The OpenHarmony backend normally receives input through the XComponent
 // callbacks that SDL_openharmony.c registers, which then call
 // SDL_OpenHarmonyDispatchTouchEvent() / DispatchMouseEvent().
 //
 // That registration happens in SDL_Init_Native_Interfaces(), which is the
 // napi module's nm_register_func (SDL_openharmony.c:523/568) -- it only runs
 // when ArkTS imports the SDL module. A host application that dlopen()s
 // libSDL3.so from native code never triggers it, so those Dispatch entry
 // points are unreachable and SDL sees no input at all.
 //
 // This is the same ownership inversion that the host-provided native window
 // change addresses on the video side: the host owns the surface *and* the
 // input pipeline, and SDL has to adopt both rather than create them.
 //
 // How it works
 // ------------
 // The host keeps its input in a lock-free ring with one read cursor per
 // consumer, and exports a pull-style accessor. We resolve that accessor (and
 // a few state queries) lazily with dlsym(RTLD_DEFAULT) and drain the ring
 // from OPENHARMONY_PumpEvents(). Two consequences worth spelling out:
 //
 //   * No new public SDL API, and no host-side change. Everything here is
 //     opportunistic: if the symbols are absent (plain SDL app, or a host that
 //     does not provide a ring) every entry point degrades to a no-op.
 //   * Draining happens on whichever thread calls SDL_PollEvent(), which is
 //     the application's main loop thread. That sidesteps the cross-thread
 //     problems you get when input is injected straight from a UI-thread
 //     callback -- SDL's event code asserts it runs on the main thread.
 //
 // Coordinate model
 // ----------------
 // The host cursor snapshot is the legacy fallback and the menu-absolute owner.
 // Once the typed backend channel is ready, grabbed relative motion arrives as a
 // dedicated AMCL_BACKEND_EVENT_RELATIVE record and must not also be derived
 // from the accumulated host cursor. Normal/menu mode continues to consume the
 // absolute snapshot. Every owner transition invalidates the legacy baseline so
 // the first fallback sample can never bridge two coordinate spaces.
 
