 // loads its library with RTLD_GLOBAL needs no cooperation from us and we do not
 // have to know what it called that library.
 //
 // That is not enough on OpenHarmony in practice. 2026-08-04 device evidence:
 // with the host library present and initialised (Minecraft was rendering
 // through it), all five RTLD_DEFAULT lookups returned NULL and this backend
 // reported "no host input bridge found after 240 attempts". The host library is
 // pulled in as a DT_NEEDED dependency of the host's own module rather than with
 // RTLD_GLOBAL, so it is reachable but not in the global scope, while SDL itself
 // was dlopen()ed from a different loader (the JVM's class loader namespace).
 //
 // Hence the fallback: ask for the library by name, but **only with RTLD_NOLOAD**.
 // This is not a stylistic choice. A plain dlopen() that actually loads the
 // library would hand us a second instance with its own event ring and its own
 // grab flag: `available` would go true, no input would ever arrive, and grab
 // writes would land on a copy nobody reads. Failing to resolve is strictly
 // better than that. RTLD_NOLOAD gives us the already-loaded instance or nothing.
 static void *OPENHARMONY_HostSym(void *host_handle, const char *name)
 {
     void *sym = dlsym(RTLD_DEFAULT, name);
     if (!sym && host_handle) {
         sym = dlsym(host_handle, name);
     }
     return sym;
 }
 
